    "text": "To determine the total number of trees Salaria has, we need to set up an equation based on the information given. Let's denote the total number of trees by \\( T \\). Since Salaria has 50% of tree A and 50% of tree B, the number of tree A is \\( \\frac{T}{2} \\) and the number of tree B is also \\( \\frac{T}{2} \\).\n\nTree A gives 10 oranges per month, and 60% of these are good. Therefore, the number of good oranges from tree A is:\n\\[ 10 \\times 0.6 = 6 \\text{ good oranges per month} \\]\n\nTree B gives 15 oranges per month, and \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\) of these are good. Therefore, the number of good oranges from tree B is:\n\\[ 15 \\times \\frac{1}{3} = 5 \\text{ good oranges per month} \\]\n\nThe total number of good oranges per month from both types of trees is the sum of the good oranges from tree A and tree B:\n\\[ 6 + 5 = 11 \\text{ good oranges per month} \\]\n\nWe know that Salaria gets 55 good oranges per month. Therefore, we can set up the following equation:\n\\[ 11 = 55 \\]\n\nThis equation is not correct as it stands, so let's re-evaluate the problem. The correct equation should be:\n\\[ 6 \\left( \\frac{T}{2} \\right) + 5 \\left( \\frac{T}{2} \\right) = 55 \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side of the equation:\n\\[ 3T + \\frac{5T}{2} = 55 \\]\n\nTo combine the terms, we need a common denominator:\n\\[ \\frac{6T}{2} + \\frac{5T}{2} = 55 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{11T}{2} = 55 \\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 2 to clear the fraction:\n\\[ 11T = 110 \\]\n\nDividing both sides by 11:\n\\[ T = 10 \\]\n\nSo, the total number of trees Salaria has is:\n\\[ \\boxed{10} \\]",
